lly weighted distant donors can still have a large count. No extrapolation guarantee follows from this statistic.\n+The weighted median is the smallest sorted residual whose cumulative weight reaches half the total. It minimizes weighted absolute residual loss, but shrinkage and clipping need not preserve that minimizer. Total weight at most \"'$10''^{-12}$ leaves the base unchanged; the separate denominator floor in Equation~'\"\\\\ref{eq:correction} applies in prediction and calibration. Queried entries are not residual-adjusted. Without an active denominator floor, effective count measures weight concentration; when active, absolute weight scale also matters. Neither case certifies geometric support or extrapolation: equally weighted distant donors can still have a large count when the floor is inactive.\n@@\n-There is no matched outer comparison that removes only residual correction while preserving all other fitting and acquisition choices. Historical tuning diagnostics are not substitutes for such an ablation. The finite donor bank, short auxiliary fits, and limited shared anchors can induce residual estimation error; fold exclusion does not ensure residual unbiasedness. Effective-count shrinkage handles weight concentration, not absolute similarity, and a history-chosen null blend cannot guarantee protection against a shifted new-model distribution. The observed control failure is therefore an important qualification of the predictor's usefulness, not grounds for a stronger mechanistic claim.\n+There is no matched outer comparison that removes only residual correction while preserving all other fitting and acquisition choices. Historical tuning diagnostics are not substitutes for such an ablation. The finite donor bank, short auxiliary fits, and limited shared anchors can induce residual estimation error; fold exclusion does not ensure residual unbiasedness. Effective-count shrinkage reflects concentration and, with an active denominator floor, absolute weigh